    {
      "tile_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting",
      "title": "Figures, Layout, and Basic Plotting",
      "summary": "Learn matplotlib's Figure/Axes object model, how to set figure size and call tight_layout, and how to use plt.plot() in both its one-argument and two-argument forms.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "matplotlib basics",
          "title": "The Figure and the Axes",
          "lede": "Every matplotlib graph lives inside two nested objects \u2014 learn which is which before writing code.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting.s01.b01",
              "role": "intro",
              "text": "Imagine this week's QuickDeliver delivery times \u2014 18, 22, 26, 19, 30 minutes \u2014 as a graph instead of a list. Matplotlib draws that graph onto two different kinds of object. Matplotlib graphs data on Figures, and each Figure can contain one or more Axes, which is the area where data is actually plotted.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#f1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting.s01.b02",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "The Figure is the overall canvas \u2014 the whole window or image you display or save. An Axes is one plotting area inside that Figure: the rectangle with its own x- and y-axis where lines, bars, or points are drawn. A single Figure can hold more than one Axes.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#f1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting.s01.b03",
              "role": "syntax",
              "text": "After `import matplotlib.pyplot as plt`, `fig, ax = plt.subplots()` creates a Figure and one Axes together: `fig` refers to the whole canvas, `ax` refers to the plotting area inside it.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "viz.matplotlib_figure_axes#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "block_id": "tile_viz_figures_layout_plotting.s01.b04",
              "diagram_kind": "container",
              "title": "Figure contains Axes",
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "figure_node",
                  "label": "Figure (canvas)",
                  "kind": "outer"
                },
                {
                  "id": "axes_node",
                  "label": "Axes (plotting area)",
                  "kind": "inner"
                },
                {
                  "id": "line_node",
